# Target C-II — a positive-density exact-normal nested rail carries phase-pure circumcenter ladders at every birth

**Date:** 2026-09-17  
**Status:** proof-only high-`t` structural synthesis; no canonical promotion claimed  
**Scope:** the corrected one-sided high-`t` good-interface population through `t=(q-k)/D<=1.8687`  
**Dependencies:** full tagged nested rail from all good interfaces; exact diameter support-normal order; local phase/bracket fusion theorem; same-host diameter exclusion  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_tagged_nested_phase_bracket_rail_t18687.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The latest frontier has three structures which were obtained in separate steps:

1. all corrected good interfaces can be placed, after one birth-host choice, on one strictly nested diameter rail of density `c0/2`;
2. the diameter directions on that rail are the **exact** ordered hull normals at the birth vertices and transport antipodally to their partner endpoints;
3. any positive-density fixed-good interface family can be thinned by a prescribed fixed fraction so that every retained interface has one adjacent two-row ladder which is simultaneously inverse-`D` near-equilateral and circumcenter-bracketing.

These statements compose without another geometric loss beyond the chosen local phase-averaging fraction. In particular, there is a positive-density **single ordered rail** for which every rail birth is equipped with the fused local ladder. Thus the remaining argument no longer has to choose between global support order and local near-triangular/circumcenter geometry.

Let

\[
c_0={113\over20{,}000{,}000}=5.65\times10^{-6}.
\tag{TNB.1}
\]

The concrete half-retention specialization below gives a nested rail of density

\[
\boxed{{c_0\over4}D-o(D)=1.4125\times10^{-6}D-o(D)}
\tag{TNB.2}
\]

whose every position carries more than `0.6436D` common adjacent-row columns, all inverse-`D` near-equilateral, including common circumcenter flanks of more than `0.05470D` columns on each side.

---

## Theorem TNB — tagged exact-normal nested rail with fused local ladders

There is a family of actual diameter bars

\[
h_s=x_sy_s,
\qquad s=1,\ldots,M,
\tag{TNB.3}
\]

born at increasing good-interface indices

\[
i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_M
\tag{TNB.4}
\]

such that

\[
\boxed{M>{c_0\over4}D-o(D).}
\tag{TNB.5}
\]

After choosing one fixed physical birth host `A`, all of the following hold simultaneously.

### A. Strictly nested exact selected-cut supports

If `I_s` is the selected-cut support of `h_s`, then

\[
\boxed{
I_1\supsetneq I_2\supsetneq\cdots\supsetneq I_M.
}
\tag{TNB.6}
\]

Every partner endpoint lies on the complementary physical destination segment

\[
Q^B\cup T_m,
\tag{TNB.7}
\]

where `B` is the opposite host.

### B. Exact diameter-normal order

Orient each bar outward at its birth endpoint,

\[
n_s={x_s-y_s\over D}.
\tag{TNB.8}
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{n_s\in N_K(x_s),\qquad -n_s\in N_K(y_s)}
\tag{TNB.9}
\]

exactly, and after one global choice of lifted host-normal orientation,

\[
\boxed{
\arg n_1<\arg n_2<\cdots<\arg n_M.
}
\tag{TNB.10}
\]

Thus the same rail is ordered simultaneously by birth layer, selected-cut support, birth support normal, and partner antipodal support normal.

### C. Fused phase-pure circumcenter ladder at every rail birth

Write the two adjacent selected rows at interface `i_s` as

\[
\tau_{i_s},\tau_{i_s+1}.
\]

There is one common transverse family `P_s` with

\[
\boxed{|P_s|>0.6436D-o(D)}
\tag{TNB.11}
\]

which is pairwise noncrossing and occurs in the same order on both rows. For every `b in P_s`, the acute strict-rhombus angular defects on **both** adjacent rows satisfy

\[
\boxed{
0<\delta(\tau_{i_s},b),\delta(\tau_{i_s+1},b)
<{7.54\times10^8\over D}+o(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{TNB.12}
\]

The same family contains common initial and terminal blocks

\[
P_s^-,P_s^+\subset P_s
\]

with

\[
\boxed{|P_s^-|,|P_s^+|>
(0.0547027-o(1))D,}
\tag{TNB.13}
\]

such that, in the monotone row-normal coordinate about the source circumcenter `o`, the crossings of every column in `P_s^-` lie below `-0.01D` on both rows, while the crossings of every column in `P_s^+` lie above `+0.01D` on both rows.

### D. Every rail position keeps its local obstruction tag

Every `s` is tagged either `EA` or `TB1`.

- At an `EA` position, `h_s` is one member of an actual exact-antipodal switching pair which positively generates the nonzero exact strip reaction `G_{i_s}`. Both pair bars retain the host-forced transverse margin `arcsin(0.4143)-o(1)` from the row/reaction axis.
- At a `TB1` position, `h_s` blocks every unit local contact-opening tangent with first-order margin at least
  \[
  (0.4143-o(1))D.
  \]

No tag is lost in the local phase/bracket thinning.

---

## 1. Start from the full tagged nested rail

The corrected good-interface fork gives

\[
|E_D|+|U_D|>c_0D-o(D),
\tag{TNB.14}
\]

where `E_D` is exact-antipodal and `U_D` is non-antipodal TB1. Choose one actual switching bar at each good interface, keeping the omitted exact-force companion as metadata on `E_D`.

The full tagged-rail theorem chooses one physical birth host and retains a family `R_D` with

\[
\boxed{|R_D|>{c_0\over2}D-o(D).}
\tag{TNB.15}
\]

All selected bars in `R_D` have distinct ordered births on that host, their partners lie in `Q^B cup T_m`, and their selected-cut supports are strictly nested. Every member of `R_D` is still a fixed-good interface, so its adjacent stopped-train terminal cost is `O(1)` and hence `o(D)` uniformly.

The exact diameter-normal theorem applies to the same selected bars and yields (TNB.9)--(TNB.10) without any further deletion.

---

## 2. Apply local phase/bracket fusion **after** support ordering

Apply the local phase/bracket fusion theorem to the already nested interface set `R_D` with

\[
\gamma={c_0\over2},
\qquad
\eta={1\over2},
\qquad
\sigma={1\over100},
\qquad
\mu={1\over100}.
\tag{TNB.16}
\]

Its retained interface density is

\[
(1-\eta)\gamma
={c_0\over4},
\]

which proves (TNB.5).

Deleting rail positions preserves strict support nesting and strict exact-normal order, so (TNB.6) and (TNB.10) survive automatically.

For these parameters the retained common column density is

\[
r-\sigma
=0.6536-0.01
=0.6436,
\tag{TNB.17}
\]

and the common flank density is

\[
\begin{aligned}
q
&=0.6536-0.01-{1.02\over\sqrt3}\\
&=0.0547027254265\ldots .
\end{aligned}
\tag{TNB.18}
\]

The phase constant is

\[
K={2C_{\rm phase}\over(1/2)(c_0/2)(0.01)}
={800C_{\rm phase}\over c_0}
=7.527802448\ldots\times10^8,
\tag{TNB.19}
\]

which gives (TNB.11)--(TNB.13).

The phase/bracket theorem deletes whole interface positions based only on their adjacent-row angular-defect charge. It does not modify the selected switching bar or its mechanism metadata. Therefore all EA/TB1 tags survive, proving Part D.

---

## 3. One mechanism survives inside the same nested fused rail

Partition the retained rail positions into

\[
R_D^{EA}\cup R_D^{TB1}.
\]

By (TNB.5), one tag occupies more than

\[
\boxed{{c_0\over8}D-o(D)
=7.0625\times10^{-7}D-o(D)}
\tag{TNB.20}
\]

positions.

Thus unconditionally the high-`t` survivor contains either

```text
an exact-force-tagged strictly nested exact-normal rail
```

or

```text
a host-strong-TB1 strictly nested exact-normal rail,
```

of density `>c0/8`, and **every position of that same rail** is equipped with its own `>0.6436D` phase-pure circumcenter-bracketing adjacent ladder.

This is stronger than separately knowing that a mechanism population, a nested rail, and fused ladders each occur at positive density.

---

## 4. A fixed physical destination can also be imposed

Same-host diameter exclusion leaves only two partner pieces for the retained rail:

\[
Q^B\quad\text{or}\quad T_m.
\]

Pigeonholing the destination after all previous structure is imposed gives one physical branch of size

\[
\boxed{{c_0\over8}D-o(D)
=7.0625\times10^{-7}D-o(D).}
\tag{TNB.21}
\]

Every bar in this fixed-destination subrail remains strictly nested and exact-normal ordered and every birth interface retains its fused local ladder.

If one additionally insists on a single EA/TB1 tag, one branch still has density

\[
\boxed{{c_0\over16}D-o(D)
=3.53125\times10^{-7}D-o(D).}
\tag{TNB.22}
\]

This yields four fully synchronized physical/mechanism possibilities, each now carrying the same types of order and local grid data:

- opposite-host + EA;
- opposite-host + TB1;
- far-terminal + EA;
- far-terminal + TB1.

At least one occurs at the density in (TNB.22).

---

## 5. Optional fixed force-pair gap without losing the fused nested form

If a fixed projective gap is needed on the EA side, use the narrow exact-force-pair capacity

\[
\#\{i\in E_D:\eta_i<\rho\}
\le2\rho D+o(D).
\tag{TNB.23}
\]

On the full fused nested rail of density `c0/4`, let `T` be its TB1 density and `E_w` its density of EA positions with force-pair gap at least `rho`. Then

\[
T+E_w
>{c_0\over4}-2\rho-o(1).
\tag{TNB.24}
\]

Hence one of these two mechanisms has density

\[
\boxed{>{c_0\over8}-\rho-o(1).}
\tag{TNB.25}
\]

For the clean choice

\[
\rho={c_0\over16},
\]

one gets either a host-strong TB1 fused nested rail or a `c0/16`-wide exact-force fused nested rail with density

\[
\boxed{{c_0\over16}D-o(D)
=3.53125\times10^{-7}D-o(D).}
\tag{TNB.26}
\]

The exact-force alternative still carries the positive two-ray reaction identity and fixed individual host transverse margin.

---

## 6. Frontier effect

The surviving high-`t` obstruction can now be required to carry, on one positive-density ordered sequence of interfaces, all of the following at once:

```text
one fixed birth host;
strictly nested exact selected-cut supports;
strictly ordered exact diameter/hull-normal directions;
exact antipodal transport of those normals to the partner boundary;
EA or strong-TB1 local obstruction metadata;
>0.6436D common adjacent-row transverse columns at every birth;
inverse-D near-equilateral angle on both adjacent rows;
>0.05470D common columns on each side of a +-0.01D circumcenter corridor.
```

The support order, boundary-normal order, and local train-grid order are therefore no longer available only on different extractions. The remaining problem is to prove an incompatibility among these three orders, or to show that one of the two physical destinations cannot carry the required antipodal normal sequence while the local near-triangular ladders persist.

No such incompatibility is asserted here. C-II remains open.

**Disposition:** synchronized the full tagged nested rail with the local inverse-`D` circumcenter-bracketing ladder. A `c0/4` nested exact-normal rail carries fused ladders at every birth; after fixing both physical destination and EA/TB1 tag, density `c0/16` remains. C-II and Level I remain open.